        }
    ]
},
{
    "name": "The Hellenic Awakening",
    "text": "247 BC\n\nYou lead the Hellenic people, heirs to a civilization whose poets, philosophers, and warriors shaped the ancient Mediterranean. Though united by language, religion, and tradition, the Greeks have rarely agreed on who should lead them. They have, however, displayed remarkable consistency in agreeing that it should be someone else.\n\nFrom the mountains of Macedon to the city-states of Hellas, every polis treasures its independence, every league pursues its own ambitions, and every alliance lasts precisely until someone remembers an old grievance. The Persian Wars may have united Greece once, but peace has always struggled to compete with politics.\n\nBeyond the Aegean, ambitious kingdoms and rising republics watch the Greek world with growing interest. Some seek allies, others seek conquest, and most eventually discover that conquering Greece is considerably easier than persuading Greeks to stop arguing with one another.\n\nYour first objective is clear.\n\nUnite [Macedonia et Hellas].",
